\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces SiliQun architecture. The simulator is organised into seven layers: the Gymnasium RL environment (\texttt  {siliqun.engine}), the Lindblad pulse solver (\texttt  {siliqun.pulse}), the OpenQASM~3.0 compiler (\texttt  {siliqun.compiler}), the physics layer with device profiles and noise channels (\texttt  {siliqun.physics}), third-party framework plugins (\texttt  {siliqun.plugins}), the numerical backend (\texttt  {siliqun.backend}), and the tensor-network primitives (\texttt  {siliqun.tensor}). An external DRL agent (e.g., PPO) interacts with SiliQun exclusively through the standard Gymnasium \texttt  {step}/\texttt  {reset} API.}}{17}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:siliqun_arch}{{4}{17}{SiliQun architecture. The simulator is organised into seven layers: the Gymnasium RL environment (\texttt {siliqun.engine}), the Lindblad pulse solver (\texttt {siliqun.pulse}), the OpenQASM~3.0 compiler (\texttt {siliqun.compiler}), the physics layer with device profiles and noise channels (\texttt {siliqun.physics}), third-party framework plugins (\texttt {siliqun.plugins}), the numerical backend (\texttt {siliqun.backend}), and the tensor-network primitives (\texttt {siliqun.tensor}). An external DRL agent (e.g., PPO) interacts with SiliQun exclusively through the standard Gymnasium \texttt {step}/\texttt {reset} API}{figure.4}{}}
